"executive_summary": "Nipah Virus (NiV) is a lethal pathogen with a case fatality rate exceeding 75%, yet no approved treatments exist. Current containment relies on isolation, and existing antivirals like ribavirin show contradictory or limited efficacy. This study addresses the lack of systematic evaluation for Nipah Virus Inhibitors (NVIs) by developing the Nipah Virus Inhibitor Knowledgebase (NVIK). The authors conducted a rigorous literature search, curating 182 NVIs, of which 98 are unique small molecule inhibitors. To prioritize these compounds for drug discovery, a combined evidence strategy was employed. This involved computing over 1800 physicochemical descriptors and comparing them against FDA-approved drugs and antiviral libraries. Additionally, compounds were filtered using PAINS and DruLito to assess toxicity and drug-likeness. An independent prioritization was performed based on the robustness of the assays used to identify the inhibitors. The results show that the NVIK library is chemically diverse, with most compounds being cyclic. While many NVIs exhibit physicochemical properties similar to FDA-approved drugs, only a few passed all DruLito filters. Specifically, NVIC0025, NVIC0032, and NVIC0048 were highlighted for passing multiple filters and showing drug-like features. The study provides a web-based platform for the community to browse, search, and submit new NVIs, aiming to accelerate the development of therapeutic interventions for NiV and potentially other viruses.",
